{
  "intro": [
    {
      "text": "One of the Silver Kingdoms of Roshar.",
      "revealedIn": ["way-of-kings"]
    },
    {
      "text": "One of the Silver Kingdoms, on the west coast of Roshar, covering much the same land as modern Shinovar and cut off from its neighbours by the Misted Mountains.",
      "revealedIn": [{ "all": ["way-of-kings", "oathbringer"] }]
    }
  ],
  "description": [
    {
      "text": "The Shin custom of naming a person as the son or daughter of a parent goes back at least to the time of the Silver Kingdoms.",
      "revealedIn": [{ "book": "oathbringer", "chapter": "38" }],
      "fromCulture": "language"
    },
    {
      "text": "As with the other Silver Kingdoms, its name reads the same backwards and forwards, a form Vorinism treats as holy. The Shin give the Heralds names of their own; Ishu-son-God and Nin-son-God are two of them.",
      "revealedIn": [
        {
          "all": [
            { "book": "edgedancer", "chapter": "9" },
            { "book": "oathbringer", "chapter": "106" }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "fromCulture": "religion"
    },
    {
      "text": "Where most of Roshar is bare stone, the ground here is mostly soil and grass.",
      "revealedIn": [
        {
          "all": [
            { "book": "way-of-kings", "chapter": "i-4" },
            { "book": "oathbringer", "chapter": "115" }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The Shin look unlike other Rosharans, pale-skinned with large, round eyes.",
      "revealedIn": [
        {
          "all": [
            { "book": "way-of-kings", "chapter": "i-4" },
            { "book": "oathbringer", "chapter": "115" }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "fromCulture": "people"
    },
    {
      "text": "The kingdom had an Oathgate, though its exact location is not known.",
      "revealedIn": ["oathbringer"]
    }
  ],
